WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
there's a good chance you've either  played flappy bird or at least you've  heard of it it's easy to play you simply  tap the screen to make the bird fly and  avoid obstacles you get points for  successfully avoiding the pipes but if  you hit them or crash into the ground  the game is over  a flappy bird was highly addictive so  much so that it ruined the life of the  guy who created it at one point dog  nguyen was making 50 000  a day on the game from ads yet he  removed it from the app store and google  play less than a year after its release  why did he walk away from what appeared  to be such a success  our story begins in hanoi vietnam in  2013. don nguyen was a 28 year old  programmer who lived with his parents he  made a living programming location  devices for taxis as a teen he was  obsessed with video games like super  mario bros and also learned to code he  played the app angry birds but thought  it was too busy there was too much going  on that inspired him to make flappy bird  over one weekend he wanted to make  something simpler that you could play  while on the go his company gears  released the game on the app store on  may 24 2013 it went live on google play  in early 2014. he decided the game would  be free he hoped to make a few hundred  bucks a month from the ads within the  game but little did he know the fortune  that lay ahead  initially flappy bird didn't attract  much attention tens of thousands of new  apps come online every month competition  was tough  however about eight months after  releasing the game  something strange happened people  started talking about it really talking  about it how they were addicted to it  how they were frustrated by it how they  couldn't stop cursing after losing  flappy bird adhered to atari co-founder  nolan bushnell's principle the belief  that all the best games are easy to  learn and difficult to master  whenever you played you felt like you  could have done better and that made you  want to try again and again the game was  all about skill  most people usually lost within the  first 10 pipes or so you got a point for  every gap he passed through if you got  10 or more points you did exceptionally  well  the physics was especially impressive  floppy bird was inspired by the act of  bouncing a ping-pong ball against a  paddle for as long as possible whenever  flappy nose dived you felt you were  along for the ride whenever flappy died  it wasn't so much that flappy died but  you died by january 2014 the game topped  the charts it was the most downloaded  app in several countries including the  us it helped that pewdiepie reviewed it  on his youtube channel there we go baby  here we go  why is it so hard  by february 2014 flappy bird had been  downloaded more than 50 million times  netting nguyen 50 000 a day in ad  revenue you'd think he'd be the happiest  man in the world  but no on february 8 2014 he tweeted  that flappy bird was ruining his simple  life and that he grew to hate his own  game  the following day he tweeted i am sorry  flappy bird users 22 hours from now i  will take flappy bird down i cannot take  this anymore  this came as a shock  why on earth would anyone remove a game  that was so insanely popular generating  50 000 a day even after apple and  google's 30 cut of the revenue  instead of being thrilled about his  success he was very very unhappy  in an interview with rolling stone a  month after taking the app offline he  said he found the sudden intention  suffocating he said his parents didn't  know about the game until the paparazzi  showed up outside his door  when journalists started reaching out to  him for interviews he pleaded with them  to give him peace  even worse than the media attention was  the criticism from some users he had  intended for the game to be played for  just a few minutes at a time as a  relaxing break but in reality that's not  how people use the app they were  addicted  he showed rolling stone a message he  received from a woman who chastised him  for distracting the children of the  world another person wrote 13 kids at my  school broke their phones because of  your game and they still play it because  it's addicting like crack nguyen  apparently took these messages to heart  quote at first i thought they were just  joking but i realized they really hurt  themselves  in an interview with forbes shortly  after taking the game down he explained  it happened to become an addictive  product i think it has become a problem  to solve that problem it's best to take  down flappy bird  on february 10 2014 flappy bird was no  more  knockoff soon flooded the app store it  was a valley effort but people still  craved the original some tried selling  their smartphones with flappy bird  already downloaded for as much as a  hundred thousand the game was still  playable there just wouldn't be any  software updates however one seller said  ebay canceled his auction because the  company policy was that the phone had to  be restored to factory settings before  it could be sold  after the demise of flappy bird fake  news stories swirled around the creator  the satirical site huzzlers claimed the  flappy bird creator had committed  suicide there were suggestions nintendo  had threatened to sue him which he  denied the popular gaming site kotaku  attacked flappy bird's creator for  allegedly ripping off nintendo's art  kotaku later ended up apologizing  the addiction the spotlight the  criticism all proved too much for nguyen  it appears money no matter how much  really can't buy happiness  nearly two years after the end of flappy  bird nguyen appeared to be in a much  better place in december 2015 he tweeted  i'm really happy these days i finally  have a team that i fit in and we work  together well not alone anymore he came  out with a bunch of other games and he  said that if users thought they were too  addicting he wouldn't hesitate to take  them down as well  but luckily for him none would come  close to the success of floppy bird  if you'd like to create your own app or  product a strong background in stem is  really important my go-to tool for  brushing up on science computer science  and math is brilliant if you'd like to  learn how to make a computer do what you  want brilliance algorithm fundamentals  course is a perfect place to start by  the end of the course you'll have an  understanding of the methods to measure  and compare performance and you'll have  mastered the fundamental problems and  algorithms brilliant is great whether  you're in high school university or if  you're already working it's full of  interactive questions like these my  personal favorite are the logic courses  in this example you have to put things  in the correct order they get harder as  you go along and build up to some truly  mind-bending challenges there are no  tests and if you ever get stuck you can  view the explanation to see where you  went wrong brilliant is free to try out  if you head to brilliant.org  newsthink my custom link is in the  description or pin comment and the first  200 people to use my link will get 20  off brilliance premium subscription  which gives you unlimited access to all  the offerings  thanks for watching for news think i'm  cindy palm

